#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2998be6e-3d61-5b63-935a-7bdb143e1005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "$-4\\pi$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2998be6e-3d61-5b63-935a-7bdb143e1005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "$-4\\pi$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232538} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc5ffdb9-90bc-5cec-a6a0-95ec2b95ac7b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232537}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "390498a7-88cc-5a78-b03f-2394c5362be8" :question/math-academy-id "q-310182" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of $y =\\sin \\left(\\pi \\left(x^{2} + 2x - 2\\right)\\right)$ at $x = 1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\sin{u}, \\qquad u = \\pi \\left(x^{2} +2x - 2\\right).\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\cos{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\pi (2x + 2).\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\cos u \\cdot \\pi (2x+2)\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (2x+2)\\cos u\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (2x+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(x^{2} +2x - 2\\right) \\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(1) & =\\pi (2\\cdot1+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(1^{2} +2\\cdot1 - 2\\right) \\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\n& =4\\pi \\cos(\\pi) \\\\[3pt]\n&= 4\\pi \\cdot (-1) \\\\[3pt]\n&= -4\\pi\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is \n\\[\nm = f'(1) = \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-4\\pi}.\n\\]"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233932 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52c44924-d8e9-5fbd-b199-533c02ea22db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "$\\pi\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232881} {:answer/id #uuid "567a7597-7b9f-400e-9a9d-1f65709be407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi\\sqrt2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186676050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52c44924-d8e9-5fbd-b199-533c02ea22db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "$\\pi\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232881} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7882970-45d5-5a12-b222-7fd793dc58e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232880}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "463cd378-4bb3-58ba-b4a9-7c4b66c3df42" :question/math-academy-id "q-342405" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of $f(x) = \\sec\\left(\\pi x^{2}\\right)$ at $x = \\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ is given by $f'\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. We express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\\[\nf = \\sec{u}, \\qquad u = \\pi x^{2}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec{u}\\tan{u}, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\pi x.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} & = \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\sec{u}\\tan{u} \\cdot 2\\pi x \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= 2\\pi x \\sec{\\left(\\pi x^{2}\\right)}\\tan{\\left(\\pi x^{2}\\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we find \n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) &= 2\\pi \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sec{\\left[\\pi\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}\\right]}\\tan{\\left[\\pi\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}\\right]} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\pi \\sec{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)} \\tan{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\pi \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\pi\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\pi\\sqrt{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "143769f0-011c-5a89-a8d1-3274d68dc818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "$-\\pi\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "143769f0-011c-5a89-a8d1-3274d68dc818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "$-\\pi\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232883} :answer-field/id #uuid "7734e075-ee26-5d23-82fa-3daceee46549" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232882} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec951508-5df0-544e-811e-06de9fdab3c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "$\\pi\\sqrt{3}+2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec951508-5df0-544e-811e-06de9fdab3c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "$\\pi\\sqrt{3}+2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232885} :answer-field/id #uuid "accc5c10-3717-5651-9bd6-da6794647764" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232884}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0be3be5b-a8d5-5056-abe1-e704e1cd0a66" :question/math-academy-id "q-342412" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x) = \\csc{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x^{3}}{6}\\right)}$ at the point $(1,2).$\n\n$y =$\n{{field-1}}$x \\: + \\:$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\\[\nf = \\csc{u}, \\qquad u = \\dfrac{\\pi x^{3}}{6}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = -\\csc{u}\\cot{u}  \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} x^{2}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\left(-\\csc{u}\\cot{u}\\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} x^{2} \\\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} x^{2} \\csc{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x^{3}}{6}\\right)\\cot{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x^{3}}{6}\\right)}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f'(1)& =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\cdot 1^2 \\cdot \\csc{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\cot{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)}} \\\\[5pt] \n\n&= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\[5pt]\n\n& = -\\pi\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,2\\right) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-y_1 &= m(x-x_1)\\\\[5pt]\n\ny-2 &= -\\pi\\sqrt{3}(x-1)\\\\[5pt]\n\ny &= \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-\\pi\\sqrt{3}}x + \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\pi\\sqrt{3} + 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233934 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f48db8cf-7b39-59af-a1cb-91ba855592e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "$-6\\sin^2{(x)}\\cos{(x)}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f48db8cf-7b39-59af-a1cb-91ba855592e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "$-6\\sin^2{(x)}\\cos{(x)}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232887} :answer-field/id #uuid "04dde564-b364-5b20-a216-7aeb3a48eaa7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232886}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45e23715-df02-5798-9f75-83ab7568fdb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-342413" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = -2\\sin^{3}{(x)},$ we have\n\n$f'(x) = $\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\\[\nf(x) = -2(\\sin{(x)})^{3}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\\[\nf = -2u^{3}, \\qquad u = \\sin{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm df}{\\textrm d u} = -6u^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n& = -6u^2 \\cdot \\cos{(x)}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-6\\sin^2{(x)}\\cos{(x)}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "489816c6-b47f-5da0-a223-5ac5074ad5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "$-7\\cos^{6}{(x)}\\sin{(x)}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "489816c6-b47f-5da0-a223-5ac5074ad5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "$-7\\cos^{6}{(x)}\\sin{(x)}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232889} :answer-field/id #uuid "31cc108a-77a1-5a3d-a2d2-0ce1e5364718" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232888}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d96ee12-4a46-5fb5-8b04-bb2200b126c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-342427" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\cos^{7}{(x)},$ we have\n\n$f'(x) = $\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\\[\nf(x) = (\\cos{(x)})^{7}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\\[\nf = u^{7}, \\qquad u = \\cos{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm d u} = 7u^6, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&= 7u^6 \\cdot (-\\sin{(x)})\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-7\\cos^6{(x)}\\sin{(x)}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
